The doctor asks me to open my mouth.
"Say ah," he says with a smile.
He looks carefully at my throat.
Afterward, he listens to my breathing.
He asks if I feel unwell anywhere else.
I tell him that my head also hurts.
"It is not a cold," he says.
"You have a common spring allergy," he explains.
I am a little surprised.
I have never had allergies before.
He tells me that it is completely normal.
The trees and flowers here are new to my body.
"You ought to buy some mild medicine at the pharmacy," the doctor says.
He promises that it will help quickly.
"Then you will feel well again," he adds.
I am very relieved.
My throat hurts, but fortunately it is not serious.
